# Meeting notes — Disaster Recovery Table Top Exercise

**Date:** _<date>_ · **Duration:** ~50 min · **Facilitator:** Kalle Hansson
**Attendees:** Dennis Landfors, Kalle Hansson, Max Netterberg, Philip Ngo, Sahba Sadeghi, Sara Landfors

> Reconstructed from the recording and lightly summarised into sections. Timestamps (elapsed meeting time) cite the moment each point was raised.

## Summary
Kalle Hansson facilitated a disaster-recovery tabletop exercise simulating the loss of the production database. All six team members participated. Sara Landfors took the incident-lead role and, with the engineering group (Dennis Landfors, Max Netterberg, Philip Ngo), walked detection, diagnosis, and recovery via restoration of the latest automated Azure backup — confirming recovery was achievable within the 12-hour RTO and that the resulting data loss sat within the 24-hour RPO. The group discussed customer communications and operating while degraded, with Kalle Hansson emphasising the plan should not slow down the actual fix. Three improvement actions were agreed.

## Details

### Introduction and scenario overview
Kalle Hansson opened the exercise, introduced the participants, and set the scenario: at approximately 14:10 on a normal weekday, customers reported that the Normain web app and API were failing, and the primary production database on Azure was unreachable with core tables missing (00:01:05). He framed it as a discussion-based exercise to validate the Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery Plan, with the group reasoning through the response together (00:02:20).

### Detection and incident declaration
The group discussed how the failure would first surface — monitoring alerts plus inbound customer reports (00:04:10). Sara Landfors said the on-call engineer would declare an incident and that she would take the incident-lead role, coordinating recovery while keeping leadership informed (00:05:35). Dennis Landfors noted the first priority was to confirm scope — which systems were affected — before making any changes (00:06:50).

### Diagnosis
Max Netterberg observed that, with no security alerting triggered, the most probable cause was an erroneous change pushed against production rather than a malicious act (00:09:15). The group agreed the immediate response would be identical either way — stabilise, restore, verify — and that it would be treated as a security breach only if evidence of compromise appeared (00:11:00). Philip Ngo added that production access should be checked to prevent further accidental writes during recovery (00:12:30).

### System recovery and backup restoration (DR)
Dennis Landfors walked through the restore: locate the most recent automated Azure backup — completed at ~02:00, roughly 12 hours earlier — and restore it to a fresh database instance rather than overwriting the damaged one (00:15:40). Max Netterberg described verifying integrity after restore via record counts and an application smoke test before cutting traffic back over (00:18:05). The group estimated full restore and verification at ~3–4 hours, comfortably inside the 12-hour RTO in Appendix B (00:20:30). Sahba Sadeghi raised that the ~12 hours of transactions between the 02:00 backup and the incident would be lost; the group confirmed this is within the 24-hour RPO and accepted (00:22:10). Sahba Sadeghi added that more frequent backups would reduce that exposure, which Kalle Hansson acknowledged as a cost trade-off to revisit as revenue grows (00:23:40).

### Customer impact and communications (BC)
Kalle Hansson raised an inbound message from an enterprise customer asking whether their data was gone and whether this was a breach (00:28:15). The group confirmed it was not a breach and agreed the CEO and Customer Support own external messaging — an honest status update, no PII, with an ETA once the restore was confirmed underway (00:30:25). Sara Landfors noted the team would send proactive customer notice rather than wait for inbound questions (00:32:00).

### Continuity while degraded
The group discussed operating during the outage — prioritising recovery work by customer impact and giving regular internal status updates until the all-clear (00:36:10). Decision authority was confirmed to rest with the incident lead, with founder sign-off for customer-impacting decisions such as service credits (00:38:20).

### Review and action items
In the wrap-up the group agreed the plan procedures were broadly accurate and recovery was achievable within targets (00:44:00). Kalle Hansson noted that, while the plan exists for good reason, the process should be simplified so it never slows down the actual fix, suggesting a lightweight first-response quick-card (00:46:15). Three actions were agreed: simplify the BC/DR plan and add a quick-card (Kalle Hansson); create a pre-approved customer outage-comms template (Sara Landfors); and revisit backup frequency as budget allows (Kalle Hansson) (00:49:30).
